\description{
Performs Fisher's exact test for testing the null of independence of rows
and columns in a contingency table with fixed marginals.
}
\details{
If \code{x} is a matrix, it is taken as a two-dimensional contingency
table, and hence its entries should be nonnegative integers.  Otherwise,
both \code{x} and \code{y} must be vectors or factors of the same length.
Incomplete cases are removed, vectors are coerced into factor objects, and
the contingency table is computed from these.

For \code{or=1} two-sided tests on 2x2, 2xk, kx2, 3xk, and kx3 tables,
computations are based on the algorithm described in Chang et al. (2015)
and extensions of it.  For larger 2xk and 3xk tables, these are combined
with two ideas from the network algorithm of Mehta and Patel (1983):
merging all partial tables which leave the same subproblem behind (via a
meet-in-the-middle split of the columns, which works well with large
counts), and reusing the results of subproblems whose fixed cells have
the same probability (which works well with small counts).  Unlike
\code{FEXACT}, which merges partial tables whose probabilities are within a
relative tolerance of about 1e-7, only exactly-equal probabilities are
merged, so the p-value is computed to nearly full double precision.
(When \code{simulate.p.value = TRUE}, all tables larger than 2x3 are instead
handled by the base R code.)  For other tests on 2x2 tables, p-values are
obtained directly using the (central or non-central) hypergeometric
distribution.

For other tables (with at least 4 rows and 4 columns), computations are
based on a C version of the FORTRAN
subroutine \code{FEXACT} which implements the network developed by Mehta and
Patel (1983, 1986) and improved by Clarkson, Fan and Joe (1993).  The
FORTRAN code can be obtained from \url{https://netlib.org/toms/643}.  Note the
latter fails (with an error message) when the entries of the table are too
large.  (It transposes the table if necessary so it has no more rows than
columns.  One constraint is that the product of the row marginals be less
than (2^31 - 1).)

For 2x2 tables, the null of conditional independence is equivalent to the
hypothesis that the odds ratio equals one.  'Exact' inference can be based
element of the contingency table has a non-central hypergeometric
distribution with non-centrality parameter given by the odds ratio
(Fisher, 1935).  The alternative for a one-sided test is based on the odds
ratio, so \code{alternative = greater} is a test of the odds ratio being bigger
than \code{or}.  Two-sided tests are based on the probabilities of the tables,
and take as "more extreme" all tables with probabilities less than or
equal to that of the observed table, the p-value being the sum of such
probabilities.

For tables handled by \code{FEXACT} and \code{hybrid = TRUE}, asymptotic chi-squared
probabilities are only used of the 'Cochran conditions' (or modified
version thereof) specified by
\code{hybridPars = c(expect = 5, percent = 80, Emin = 1)} are satisfied, that
is if no cell has expected counts less than 1 (\verb{= Emin}) and more than 80\%
(\verb{= percent}) of the cells have expected counts at least 5 (\verb{= expect}),
otherwise the exact calculation is used.  A corresponding \verb{if()} decision
is made for all sub-tables considered.

For tables handled by \code{FEXACT}, internal tables can be too large for the
exact test in which case an error is signalled.  Apart from increasing
\code{workspace} sufficiently, which then may lead to very long running times,
using \code{simulate.p.value = TRUE} may then often be sufficient and hence
advisable.

2xk and 3xk tables are not subject to the \code{workspace} limit.  Their
memory use grows with the difficulty of the problem: it is usually well
under 50 MB, and internal caps bound it to roughly 250 MB for tables with
up to ~20 columns (growing slowly with more columns), which is only
approached by problems taking many seconds (e.g. a 3x5 table with total
450 and p ~= 0.02 takes ~15 seconds and ~180 MB).  Running time grows
quickly with the number of columns and the table total, especially when
the p-value is extremely small; in the latter case, note that p-values
below ~1e-323 are computed much more quickly when \code{log.p = FALSE}, since
they're just reported as zero.

Running time is hard to predict in advance for these tables (it can
vary by orders of magnitude between tables of similar shape and total),
so instead of refusing hopeless problems up front, the computation is
abandoned with an error once it has run for \code{time.limit} seconds (one
hour by default); \code{simulate.p.value = TRUE} is then usually the best
option.  These computations can also be interrupted in the usual way
(e.g. with Ctrl-C).  \code{time.limit} does not apply to tables handled by
\code{FEXACT}.

Simulation is done conditional on the row and column marginals, and works
only if the marginals are strictly positive.  (A C translation of the
algorithm of Patefield (1981) is used.)  Note that the default number of
replicates (\code{B = 2000}) implies a minimum p-value of about 0.0005
(1/(\code{B}+1)).
}
\examples{
## Agresti (1990, p. 61f; 2002, p. 91) Fisher's Tea Drinker
## A British woman claimed to be able to distinguish whether milk or
##  tea was added to the cup first.  To test, she was given 8 cups of
##  tea, in four of which milk was added first.  The null hypothesis
##  is that there is no association between the true order of pouring
##  and the woman's guess, the alternative that there is a positive
##  association (that the odds ratio is greater than 1).
TeaTasting <-
matrix(c(3, 1, 1, 3),
       nrow = 2,
       dimnames = list(Guess = c("Milk", "Tea"),
                       Truth = c("Milk", "Tea")))
fisher.test(TeaTasting, alternative = "greater")
## => p = 0.2429, association could not be established

## Fisher (1962, 1970), Criminal convictions of like-sex twins
Convictions <- matrix(c(2, 10, 15, 3), nrow = 2,
	                 dimnames = list(c("Dizygotic", "Monozygotic"),
	                         c("Convicted", "Not convicted")))
Convictions
fisher.test(Convictions, alternative = "less")
fisher.test(Convictions, conf.int = FALSE)
(fisher.test(Convictions, conf.level = 0.95) -> ft)$conf.int
 fisher.test(Convictions, conf.level = 0.99)$conf.int

## Some r x c tables with many small expected counts, so the chi-squared
## approximation is inadvisable.  R 4.6's algorithm can't handle these within
## a minute, even with workspace = 64000000 (it runs for about two minutes on
## the first two, and fails with a hash key overflow on the third), but they
## complete within a fraction of a second with the new algorithm.
result <- fisher.test(matrix(c(297, 209, 322, 218, 145, 226, 7, 4, 3,
                               394, 260, 406, 2, 4, 3), nrow=3))
result$p.value
fisher.test(matrix(c(166, 141, 110, 2, 0, 0, 4, 6, 3,
                     160, 138, 108, 173, 163, 118, 82, 73, 53), nrow=3))
fisher.test(matrix(c(212, 992, 1099, 53, 339, 373, 84, 374, 469,
                     0, 2, 3), nrow=3))

}
